<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" data-theme="dark">
  <head>
    <title><%= content_for(:title) || "Chopshop Studio" %> · Chopshop</title>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow">
    <%= csrf_meta_tags %>
    <%= csp_meta_tag %>
    <%= stylesheet_link_tag "application", "palette-tool", "job-bands", "data-turbo-track": "reload" %>
    <%= javascript_importmap_tags(current_user ? "application" : "login") %>
  </head>

  <body>
    <% if current_user %>
      <nav class="topbar">
        <div class="container nav-content">
          <a href="<%= root_path %>" class="logo">
            <div class="logo-icon">✂</div>
            <div class="logo-text">Chopshop<span>Studio</span></div>
          </a>
          <ul class="nav-links">
            <li><a href="<%= root_path %>">Projects</a></li>
            <li><a href="<%= json_path %>">JSON</a></li>
            <li><a href="<%= flux_path %>">FLUX</a></li>
            <li><a href="<%= jev_path %>">JEV</a></li>
            <%# ONE entry for ONE service: BP is Blend-Plot, the two halves -- plot
                (documents) and blend (3D) -- on one page. It replaced the separate
                PLOT and BLENDER links; /plot and /blender redirect here so nothing
                that already pointed at them 404s. %>
            <li><a href="<%= bp_path %>">BP</a></li>
            <%# DATASETS was the last entry here before it left the row for the
                same reason STATUS did: a reference surface, and the row does
                not grow for a page that cannot act on a job. It went to the
                surface that already owns its subject — a POPUP on FLUX,
                because a prompt is taken FROM the library and generated WITH
                flux — one subject, one page.
                The nav is the working path: Projects · JSON · FLUX · JEV ·
                BP, oldest to newest. %>
          </ul>
          <div class="nav-side">
            <%# THE CENSUS LINE (#10) — the kb's right-aligned mono status line
                ("214 jobs · 37 candidates · 12 projects"), backported. ONE
                helper (CensusHelper#census_line), ONE slot: the header's
                right-hand chrome, beside the runners menu and the logout, so
                every page states its own scale at a glance. The helper renders
                an EMPTY string where a page has no corpus to count (a form, a
                login), so the header never carries a dead "0 jobs". The views no
                longer render it themselves — this slot is the only one, so the
                line and the page it describes cannot disagree. %>
            <%= census_line %>
            <%# The runners a NEW job can be filed against (Job.form_runners), not
                every configured label: the legacy `plot`/`blender` kinds are
                superseded by `bp` and no longer offered, so listing them here
                showed a runner the operator cannot choose and cannot explain.
                A legacy label still serves the jobs already filed against it —
                it is just not a thing this menu should claim is on offer. %>
            <% health = Job.form_runners.map { |r| [r, RunnerClient.health(Job.runners[r])] } %>
            <details class="runner-menu">
              <summary class="runner-pill"><span class="dot"></span>runners ▾</summary>
              <div class="runner-dropdown">
                <% health.each do |name, h| %>
                  <div class="runner-row">
                    <span class="dot <%= h && h[:ok] ? 'dot-ok' : 'dot-down' %>"></span>
                    <span class="mono"><%= name %></span>
                    <span class="runner-status"><%= h && h[:ok] ? "up" : "down" %></span>
                  </div>
                <% end %>
              </div>
            </details>
            <%= button_to "Log out", logout_path, method: :delete, class: "btn btn-secondary btn-sm" %>
          </div>
        </div>
      </nav>

      <%# DATA-LAYER HEALTH BANNER (#3) — the kb's `.banner.warn` under the
          header, backported. One line, amber, listing the audit's unresolved
          checks and linking to /audit. Rendered on EVERY authed page (the
          header is the one place every page shares), fed by AuditGaps.banner —
          a projection of the SAME report /audit renders, so the banner and the
          page cannot disagree. It renders nothing when the data layer is clean.
          Gated on current_user like the nav it follows: /audit is login-gated
          and the report is this owner's own debt, so an anonymous visitor is
          offered neither the banner nor the page it links to. %>
      <%= render "shared/data_health_banner" %>

    <%# Scrapbook: a left sidebar drawer of favourited sources/inverts/candidates/
        proofs. It is its own store (storage/scrapbook), so it survives deleting
        the jobs its contents came from. Rendered on every authed page; the toggle
        is the fixed handle on the left edge. %>
    <%# The favourite button, defined ONCE for the whole app: this is the same
        partial the tiles and the rows render, cloned into the preview popup's
        toolbox by aether.js (csFavButtonNode). Rendering it here instead of
        writing a second button in JavaScript is what keeps the star, its
        data-* contract and the one delegated click handler identical wherever
        a favourite appears. The template's own job/name are filled in by the
        popup, so it ships empty. %>
    <template id="csFavTemplate">
      <%= render "scrapbook/fav_button", job_id: "", name: "", label: "this file", word: "Favourite" %>
    </template>
    <button type="button" class="scrapbook-toggle" id="scrapbookToggle"
            title="Scrapbook" aria-label="Open Scrapbook" aria-expanded="false"
            aria-controls="scrapbookSidebar">
      <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><polygon points="12 2 15.09 8.26 22 9.27 17 14.14 18.18 21.02 12 17.77 5.82 21.02 7 14.14 2 9.27 8.91 8.26 12 2"></polygon></svg>
    </button>
    <aside class="scrapbook-sidebar" id="scrapbookSidebar" aria-hidden="true" aria-label="Scrapbook">
      <header class="scrapbook-head">
        <h2 class="scrapbook-title">Scrapbook</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="scrapbook-body" id="scrapbookBody">
        <p class="scrapbook-empty">Nothing favourited yet.</p>
      </div>
    </aside>

    <%# Composite sidebar: the frame's SECOND panel, a vertical partner to the
        scrapbook drawer.
        #frame-vertical-stack — same left column, bottom-origin, and it becomes
        a two-band column when both panels are up (body.frame-split).
        Not a second store: it is the NAVIGATION frame over the composites
        table, filled from /composite/list, and every row opens the builder at
        /composite?id=N.
    #frame-vertical-stack %>
    <aside class="scrapbook-sidebar composite-sidebar" id="compositeSidebar" aria-hidden="true" aria-label="Composites">
      <header class="scrapbook-head">
        <h2 class="scrapbook-title">Composites</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="scrapbook-body" id="compositeBody">
        <p class="scrapbook-empty">No composites yet.</p>
      </div>
    </aside>

    <%# The frame's ONE cycle control — a single ✕ orb on the column's outer
        edge, vertically centred, that cycles the frame's modes: full Scrapbook
        -> half and half -> full Composites -> close (double-click closes the
        frame outright). It is a SIBLING of both panels rather than a child of
        either, because it controls the FRAME: an orb per panel was two buttons
        where the operator wanted one, and in the both-up state it showed
        twice. Visible whenever the frame is open (body.scrapbook-open), hidden
        when it is closed — the star returns then, and the star is the way back
        in. The ✕ is an inline SVG like every other close in the app, and the
        ring + living light behind it reuse the drawer's orb accent and the
        login sunspot recipe (pinned by FavouriteOrbTest). The drawer's own
        close and the popup standard (× in .popup-head) are separate
        conventions; this is the FRAME's control. %>
    <button type="button" class="frame-cycle" id="frameCycle"
            title="Cycle side panels: Scrapbook, both, Composites, close (double-click closes)"
            aria-label="Cycle side panels">
      <span class="scrapbook-close-orb" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="login-sun-layer" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="login-sun-burst" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="login-sun-pink" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><line x1="18" y1="6" x2="6" y2="18"></line><line x1="6" y1="6" x2="18" y2="18"></line></svg>
    </button>
    <% end %>

    <main class="container">
      <% if notice %><div class="flash notice"><%= notice %></div><% end %>
      <% if alert %><div class="flash alert"><%= alert %></div><% end %>
      <%= yield %>
    </main>

    <footer class="footer-note">
      <div class="container footer-inner">
        <span>chopshop-svg · trace → you pick → proof · the pipeline never picks a winner</span>
        <%# The bar's right-hand chrome: the About link with the check-engine
            light beside it. Wrapped so the two travel TOGETHER at the right
            edge -- .footer-inner spreads its children with space-between, so a
            bare third child would have parked the light in the middle of the
            bar, away from the link it belongs with.
            #engine-light-link — the footer's route to /status. %>
        <div class="footer-chrome">
          <%# The footer's reference BUTTON — the About page, labelled "about".
              It carried the placeholder "???" until 2026-10-20 (#101). A pill at
              the page-action footprint, so it reads as chrome rather than as the
              mono caption the bar is. Its extra width is .footer-demo's own
              horizontal padding, not spaces in the label (HTML collapses those).
              %>
          <a href="<%= about_path %>" class="footer-demo">about</a>
          <%# AUDIT (#95) — the data-debt count, in the same chrome slot STATUS
              took when it left the header. The header nav is the WORKING path
              (Projects · JSON · FLUX · JEV · BP) and does not grow for a page
              that cannot act on a job; the footer bar is where the reference
              surfaces live, and the audit is reachable from every page rather
              than from one. Same pill as the About link, so the two read as one
              family of chrome. Gated on current_user for the same reason the
              engine light is: the bar renders on /login too, and the page it
              leads to reports this owner's own records. %>
          <% if current_user %>
            <a href="<%= audit_path %>" class="footer-demo">audit</a>
            <%# GRAPH (#96) — the studio's own records as one shape: projects,
                jobs, candidates and proofs joined by their containment and pick
                lines. In the SAME chrome slot as the audit pill, for the same
                reason: it is a reference surface reached from every page rather
                than from one, and the header nav (the working path) does not
                grow for it. Gated on current_user like the pills beside it —
                the bar renders on /login too, and the graph is this owner's own
                records plus their login-gated artwork. %>
            <a href="<%= graph_path %>" class="footer-demo">graph</a>
            <%# The check-engine light: a round orb carrying no word at all, just
                an engine glyph and a status dot, and it LINKS to the STATUS page
                rather than opening anything -- the telemetry and the ML/ranking
                configuration behind it are that page's own panels.
                It is the only way to STATUS: the link left the sticky header,
                which is also what keeps the bar's link row at six entries
                rather than growing a seventh.
                Gated on current_user because the BAR renders on every page,
                /login included, and the page it leads to reports the signed-in
                owner's own signal. %>
            <a class="engine-light <%= engine_light_cold? ? 'is-cold' : 'is-live' %>"
               href="<%= status_path %>"
               title="Engine / telemetry — <%= engine_light_state %>"
               aria-label="Engine / telemetry — <%= engine_light_state %>">
              <svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M14.7 6.3a1 1 0 0 0 0 1.4l1.6 1.6a1 1 0 0 0 1.4 0l3.77-3.77a6 6 0 0 1-7.94 7.94l-6.91 6.91a2.12 2.12 0 0 1-3-3l6.91-6.91a6 6 0 0 1 7.94-7.94l-3.76 3.76z"></path></svg>
              <span class="engine-light-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </a>

            <%# REPORT A PROBLEM. The maintenance worker, in the same round-orb
                family as the check-engine light beside it: the two are the same
                kind of footer control, small and quiet and always there. It is
                a BUTTON and not a link because it opens a popup rather than
                navigating — an operator describes a problem from the page they
                hit it on, and that page travels with the report because the
                controller reads it off the request.

                It sits INSIDE this gate rather than in one of its own: the bar
                renders on /login too, and filing an issue is not a thing a
                visitor can do. Its title carries the other half of the state
                the operator cannot see — whether filing is even configured on
                this box — so nobody types a careful report into a box that
                cannot send it. That flag is also stated inside the popup. %>
            <button type="button" class="engine-light maint-light"
                    data-popup="bug-report-popup"
                    title="Report a problem, an expectation or a suggestion<%= bug_reporting_configured? ? '' : ' — filing is not configured on this box' %>"
                    aria-label="Report a problem, an expectation or a suggestion">
              <%= image_tag "maint-worker-32.png", alt: "", class: "maint-light-icon" %>
            </button>
          <% end %>
        </div>
      </div>
    </footer>

    <%# The report popup rides at the END of the document, beside the welcome
        overlay, rather than inside the footer: the footer is inside a
        `container` whose width the popup panel would inherit, and a modal that
        is narrower than the panel it draws is a layout bug waiting to happen.
        Gated on current_user for the same reason its button is — and so the
        markup cannot leak the form onto the login page. %>
    <% if current_user %>
      <%= render "bug_reports/popup" %>
    <% end %>

    <% if flash[:welcome] %>
      <div class="welcome-overlay" id="welcomeOverlay" role="dialog" aria-label="Welcome">
        <div class="welcome-type" id="welcomeType" aria-live="polite"></div>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <%# Frame cycle: the close orb cycles the frame's states.
        #frame-cycle-orb — the state machine lives in app/javascript/aether.js
        (frameApply / frameCycle), NOT here: an inline script cannot survive a
        Turbo body swap, and it cannot share `frameState` with the drawer's own
        open/close. The order is star -> scrap -> both -> comp -> none, chosen so
        every state is reachable from a control that is still on screen in it. %>
  </body>
</html>
